 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** The server pushes committed events over persistent WebSocket connections rather than waiting for browser polls or delegating to a hosted realtime service. Polling (five-second interval) was rejected because it adds 0–5 s of lag and multiplies read load at opening-time burst rates. A hosted realtime service (e.g. Pusher, Ably) was rejected to avoid recurring vendor fees and provider coupling. Self-hosted WebSockets add connection-management and reconnect complexity — the change store must maintain a publication outbox, the delivery layer must track per-session positions, and browsers must implement catch-up on reconnect — but deliver subsecond latency with no third-party dependency. See `docs/decisions/delivery-approach.md`.
